          narration: "Sooner or later, someone will ask you the big question: how big is your market? The standard framework is TAM, SAM and SOM, and it sounds like alphabet soup until you see the logic. TAM, the total addressable market, is total demand for this kind of product if you captured everyone, everywhere. SAM, the serviceable available market, is the slice your particular business model, geography and product could actually serve. And SOM, the serviceable obtainable market, is the share of that serviceable market you can realistically capture in the near term, given your resources, competitors and sales capacity. So: TAM is everything, SAM is what you could serve, and SOM is what you can actually win soon. How do you estimate them? Top-down starts with a big published figure and trims it by percentages, and it tends to produce fantasy numbers. I've seen pitches claim one percent of a gigantic market as if it were a law of nature. Bottom-up starts from units you understand: the number of real customers you can identify, multiplied by what each would pay. Investors trust bottom-up far more, because every number can be checked and challenged.",
