          heading: "Interviewing customers: the Mom Test",
          narration: "Now for the most important skill this week, and possibly this half of the semester: interviewing customers properly. Here's the problem. People are polite. Describe your idea and ask whether they'd buy it, and most people will smile and say yes, sounds great, because they don't want to hurt your feelings. My own mother told me my lecture-notes app was wonderful. She has never taken a lecture note in her life. Those answers are hypothetical, and hypothetical answers are close to worthless as evidence. Rob Fitzpatrick's book The Mom Test gives a simple set of rules to dodge this trap. Talk about their life, not your idea. Ask about specific things they've done in the past, rather than generalisations or opinions about the future. And talk less, listen more. So instead of asking whether they'd use an app for splitting bills, ask them to tell you about the last time they split a bill with housemates. What happened? What tool did they use? What went wrong? Have they ever looked for something better? Past behaviour is evidence. Compliments, vague enthusiasm and promises about the future are not. Write that on a sticky note.",
