          narration: "Hello, hello, welcome back! Last week you got out of the building, asked about past behaviour instead of collecting compliments, and designed an MVP to learn fast. So, how did the weekend experiments go? Don't worry, nobody's first experiment is pretty. Today we take what you've learned and turn it into a value proposition. A value proposition is the reason a specific customer should choose your offer over the alternatives. Every part of that sentence earns its place. A specific customer, because value differs from person to person. Choose your offer, because they have to actually act. And over the alternatives, because value is always relative. Your product isn't valuable in a vacuum; it's valuable compared with what the customer would otherwise do, including competitors, workarounds or nothing at all. So a strong value proposition focuses on a particular customer, their problem, and the measurable benefit you deliver compared with their current option. A weak one lists every feature, talks about the founders' backgrounds, or describes the tech stack. I've never met a customer who cared which framework I used. They care that they'll save three hours a week, avoid a fine, or stop losing sales.",
