          heading: "Financial projections and break-even",
          narration: "Financial projections show how revenue, costs and cash are expected to develop, usually over three to five years. Here's a secret: for an early startup, nobody expects them to be accurate. What reviewers look for is whether your assumptions are reasonable and clearly stated. How many customers, at what price, with what churn, and what costs? A key tool here is break-even analysis, which tells you how many units you must sell to cover your costs. Remember fixed and variable costs from last week? Here they come. Each unit you sell contributes its price minus its variable cost, and that's called the contribution margin. Break-even volume is fixed costs divided by the contribution margin per unit. Let's try one together. Suppose fixed costs are ninety thousand dollars a year, you sell a product for forty dollars, and each unit costs ten dollars to deliver. The contribution margin is forty minus ten, thirty dollars. Ninety thousand divided by thirty is three thousand units a year. Sell fewer and you make a loss; above that, every extra unit adds thirty dollars of profit. One last warning. Track your cash flow too, because even profitable startups can run out of cash while waiting for customers to pay.",
