          heading: "Public speaking",
          narration: "Delivery matters as much as content, and I have wonderful news. Public speaking is a skill, not a personality trait, and it improves with deliberate practice. I was a mumbling, slide-reading presenter for years before a colleague filmed me and made me watch. Painful, but transformative. So, rehearse out loud several times, ideally in front of people who'll give honest feedback, and time yourself. Know your opening and closing lines by heart, because nerves are worst at the start, and the close is what people remember. Signpost so the audience always knows where they are. Say, there are three reasons, and then count them off. Slow down. Nervous speakers rush, and a brief pause before a key point makes it land. Like this. See? Make eye contact with different parts of the room. Stand fairly still rather than pacing, and use natural gestures. If you lose your place, pause, glance at your slide, and carry on; the audience rarely notices. In a team pitch, plan smooth handovers and agree who answers which kinds of questions. And finally, treat nerves as energy. Almost every experienced presenter still feels them. I still do, every first lecture of semester.",
