          narration: "Fairness sounds simple. Treat people fairly, done. But in AI, it's surprisingly slippery. Researchers have proposed several mathematical definitions. One asks that different groups receive positive outcomes at similar rates. Another asks that the model's error rates, such as false positives, be similar across groups. Another asks that a given score means the same likelihood for every group. And here's the catch that kept me up at night when I first read it: researchers have shown that, except in special cases, you can't satisfy all of these at once. This came to wide attention through a debate over a criminal risk assessment tool in the United States, where journalists and the tool's makers each argued it was fair or unfair, using different definitions. Both sides had a point. So fairness isn't purely a technical setting you toggle on. It's a value choice about which kind of fairness matters most in a particular context, and it should involve the people affected, not just the engineers. In practice, fairness work means testing performance separately for different groups, investigating any gaps, and documenting the trade-offs you've chosen and why. If someone tells you their model is simply fair, ask them: by which definition?",
