          narration: "Four related principles turn up in almost every AI ethics framework, and they work like a chain. Transparency means being open that an AI system is being used, and about how it works, what data it uses, and its limitations. Explainability means people can understand how the system reached a decision about them, in terms meaningful to them. A loan applicant needs to know which factors mattered, not the model's internal weights. Techniques range from using simpler, interpretable models, to producing explanations for complex ones, such as showing which inputs most influenced a result, or what would need to change for a different outcome. Contestability means people affected by an AI decision can challenge it, through a timely process with a human able to review and overturn the outcome. And notice how the chain connects. You can't challenge a decision you don't understand, so explanations make contestability possible. Finally, accountability means identifiable people and organisations are responsible for the system's outcomes across its whole life cycle. I have one rule for my students here. Never, ever say the algorithm decided. Someone chose to build it, someone chose to deploy it, and someone chose to rely on it. That someone might one day be you.",
