          heading: "Barriers, culture and inclusion",
          narration: "Many barriers get in the way of understanding. Semantic barriers include jargon and acronyms the listener doesn't share. Ever explained an API to your grandmother? Psychological barriers include stress, defensiveness and assumptions. Physical barriers include noise, poor audio and time zones. And cultural barriers matter in Australia's diverse workplaces and in global teams. The anthropologist Edward Hall distinguished high-context cultures, where much meaning is carried by relationships and implied cues, from low-context cultures, where meaning is stated explicitly in words. Australian workplace culture tends toward the direct, low-context end. A colleague from a higher-context background might signal disagreement indirectly, perhaps by saying something could be difficult, and a direct communicator might completely miss it. I grew up between two cultures, and I've been on both sides of that misunderstanding. Neither style is wrong; the risk is misreading each other. Inclusion means creating conditions where everyone can contribute. In practice, invite quieter team members to speak, avoid idioms and in-jokes that exclude people, rotate meeting times across time zones, and check understanding rather than assuming silence means agreement. Inclusive teams catch more problems, because more perspectives get heard. Diversity without inclusion is just a photo on a website.",
