          heading: "Active listening",
          narration: "The most underrated communication skill is listening. Not hearing. Listening. Active listening means fully concentrating on the speaker, understanding their message, and showing that you've understood. It's different from waiting politely for your turn to talk, which, if I'm honest, is what I did for most of my twenties. Several techniques make it concrete. Give your attention: face the person, put your phone away, and don't multitask. Paraphrase: restate their point in your own words, such as, so the main issue is that the client changes priorities every sprint, is that right? Ask clarifying questions, like, what would a good outcome look like for you? Summarise at the end to confirm you share the same understanding. And hold back judgement and solutions until you've understood the problem. Common failures include preparing your rebuttal while the other person is still speaking, jumping in with a fix too early, and interrupting. Why does this matter so much? In requirements gathering, misunderstanding is expensive. In conflict, people need to feel heard before they'll move an inch. And in customer discovery, back in week three, it's how you collect real evidence instead of hearing what you expected. One skill, three superpowers.",
