          heading: "Negotiation: interests and BATNA",
          narration: "Negotiation happens constantly at work: scope with clients, deadlines with managers, salary with employers. The most influential framework comes from Roger Fisher and William Ury's book Getting to Yes, which describes principled negotiation. Its first idea is to separate the people from the problem, so disagreement doesn't become personal. The second is to focus on interests, not positions. A position is what someone says they want; an interest is why they want it. Here's a classic illustration. Two people argue over one orange. Splitting it in half seems fair, until you discover one wanted the juice and the other wanted the peel for baking. Understanding interests revealed a solution that fully satisfied both. Lovely, isn't it? The third idea is to invent options for mutual gain, and the fourth is to use objective criteria, like market rates or industry standards. And finally, know your BATNA, your best alternative to a negotiated agreement. It's what you'll do if this negotiation fails. Your BATNA tells you when to walk away, and improving it strengthens your position. When I negotiated my first salary, I had no BATNA at all, and I accepted the first number offered. Don't be young Kofi, again.",
