          heading: "The Thomas-Kilmann conflict styles",
          narration: "Conflict in teams is normal, and some conflict about ideas is healthy, because it tests assumptions. The goal isn't zero conflict; it's handling it well. The Thomas-Kilmann model describes five conflict-handling styles along two dimensions. Assertiveness is how much you try to satisfy your own concerns. Cooperativeness is how much you try to satisfy the other person's concerns. Now picture a grid. Competing is high assertiveness and low cooperativeness: pursuing your own position, sometimes at the other person's expense. Accommodating is the reverse, low assertiveness and high cooperativeness: yielding to the other side. Avoiding is low on both: sidestepping or postponing the issue. Collaborating is high on both: working together to find a solution that fully satisfies everyone's underlying concerns. Remember the orange? That's collaborating. And compromising sits in the middle, moderate on both: each side gives up something to reach an acceptable middle ground. Here's a question for self-reflection. Which style do you fall back on when you're tired and stressed? Mine is avoiding, I'm afraid. A key insight is that no style is always right. Each fits different situations, and skilled professionals choose deliberately rather than defaulting to habit.",
