          heading: "Choosing a conflict style",
          narration: "So when is each style the right one? Let's go through them. Collaborating is best when the issue matters to both sides and there's time to explore interests, such as agreeing on an architecture the whole team will live with for years. Competing can be right in an emergency, or when a firm ethical or safety line is at stake. Remember week seven? You shouldn't compromise on shipping code with a known security flaw. That's a line, not a negotiation. Accommodating makes sense when the issue matters far more to the other person than to you, or when the relationship matters more than the point. Avoiding can be wise when tempers are high and everyone needs time to cool down, or when the issue is trivial, but chronic avoidance lets problems fester. Trust me on that one. Compromising is useful under time pressure when full collaboration isn't possible. In team conflicts, start with active listening to understand each person's interests, focus on the problem rather than personalities, and agree next steps in writing. And if a conflict becomes personal or involves misconduct, escalate it to a manager or another appropriate channel. That's not weakness. That's professionalism.",
