          heading: "Decision-making and prioritisation",
          narration: "Professionals make decisions under uncertainty constantly. A simple rational process is to define the problem, gather relevant information, generate options, evaluate them against agreed criteria, decide, and review the result later. Writing down your criteria before comparing options reduces bias, just like writing the success signal before an experiment back in week three. Watch out for groupthink, where a cohesive team suppresses dissent to keep the peace and makes poor decisions as a result. Appointing someone to argue the opposite case, or asking everyone to write their view before discussion, helps counter it. For prioritising your own work, the Eisenhower matrix is wonderfully simple. It sorts tasks by two questions: is it urgent, and is it important? Important and urgent tasks, like a production outage, you do now. Important but not urgent tasks, like learning, planning and refactoring, you schedule, and this quadrant is where long-term success lives. Urgent but unimportant tasks, like many interruptions, you delegate or minimise. And tasks that are neither? You drop them, guilt-free. Here's the sad truth. Many people spend their days on urgent tasks and never reach the important ones. I keep my matrix on a sticky note on my monitor. It's saved my sanity more than once.",
