          heading: "Teamwork and bringing it together",
          narration: "Teams don't start out effective. Bruce Tuckman's widely used model describes stages of team development: forming, when people are polite and unsure; storming, when disagreements surface; norming, when the team agrees on ways of working; and performing, when it works smoothly toward its goals. A later stage, adjourning, covers the team wrapping up. Storming is normal, not a sign of failure, and today's tools help you through it. Agree on norms early: how you communicate, how decisions are made, and how conflict is raised. Make roles clear. Give feedback that's specific, timely and about behaviour rather than personality. And build psychological safety, so people can admit mistakes and raise concerns without fear. That links back to everything this semester. Ethical issues only get raised in teams where people feel safe to speak. Startups only succeed when founders communicate honestly. And your career will be shaped as much by how you work with people as by what you know. Well, we've reached our own adjourning stage. It has been a genuine joy teaching you. Go build things people need, put your name only on work you're proud of, and keep the public interest first. Goodbye for now, and good luck!",
